  "prompt_text": "This schematic illustrates how platelets and platelet-derived extracellular vesicles may promote neutrophil-driven thrombosis through HMGB1 signaling. At the top, platelets and their small released vesicles are shown carrying HMGB1. In the central concept, these HMGB1-positive platelets/vesicles interact with neutrophils through the receptor RAGE, leading to neutrophil activation.\n\nThe activated neutrophils appear to undergo two linked responses: granule mobilization and autophagy induction. These processes converge on NET formation, shown as the release of long web-like extracellular strands from neutrophils. The figure emphasizes that this NET production contributes to immunothrombosis, placing neutrophils at the center of a thrombo-inflammatory mechanism triggered by platelet-derived signals.\n\nThe lower portion visually reinforces this outcome by depicting several neutrophils connected by extensive blue NET-like fibers, with surrounding dots and labeled intracellular components suggesting released granule contents and autophagy-related structures. A legend identifies key elements such as autophagosomes, myeloperoxidase, HMGB1, RAGE, and platelet-derived extracellular vesicles. Overall, the message is that platelet-associated HMGB1 seems to activate neutrophils via RAGE, promoting autophagy-linked NET release and thereby fostering immunothrombosis.",
